For finite job and machine sets \(J\) and \(M\), let \(E_{jm}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}\) be the measurable times when job \(j\) executes on machine \(m\). A schedule permits migratory processing when it may assign a job to more than one machine, so \(E_{jm}
eq\varnothing\) and \(E_{jm'}
eq\varnothing\) can hold for distinct \(m,m'\), subject to \(E_{jm}\cap E_{jm'}=\varnothing\) up to measure zero. Feasibility also requires \(\sum_{m\in M}\mu(E_{jm})=p_j\) for every \(j\) and pairwise disjoint machine-occupancy sets \(E_{jm}\cap E_{km}=\varnothing\) for \(j
eq k\).
